.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-PKG-WRAPPER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-pkg-wrapper
.Nd take a snapshot before apt or dnf changes the system, and give both a rollback command
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm apt | apt-get | dnf | dnf5 | yum
.Op Ar arguments ...
.Nm apt | dnf
.Cm rollback
.Op Ar rollback-arguments ...
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The installer links
.Pa /usr/local/bin/apt ,
.Pa apt-get ,
.Pa dnf ,
.Pa dnf5
and
.Pa yum
to
.Nm ,
for each of them the system has.
.Pa /usr/local/bin
comes before
.Pa /usr/bin
in the login path and in
.Xr sudo 8 Ns 's ,
so the usual commands reach it.
It looks at the name it was called by and the command's verb \(em the
first word that is not an option, skipping the value of an option that
takes one \(em and:
.Bl -bullet
.It
for
.Cm rollback ,
runs
.Xr kldload-rollback 8
with the rest of the arguments; with none it runs
.Ql kldload-rollback last --no-reboot ,
which stages the rollback and does not restart;
.It
for a verb that changes the system, run as root, takes a
.Ql apt-pre
or
.Ql dnf-pre
snapshot of the boot environment first;
.It
for
.Ql apt upgrade ,
.Ql full-upgrade
or
.Ql dist-upgrade
with packages held, first explains on standard error why apt will say
they were
.Dq kept back ;
.El
.Pp
and then runs the real command from
.Pa /usr/bin
with the arguments untouched, so its output and exit status are the real
command's.
.Pp
The verbs that change the system differ: for apt
.Ql update
only refreshes the package lists, while for dnf it upgrades.
apt:
.Cm install , remove , purge , upgrade , full-upgrade , dist-upgrade ,
.Cm autoremove , reinstall , satisfy , build-dep .
dnf:
.Cm install , upgrade , update , remove , erase , downgrade , reinstall ,
.Cm distro-sync , distrosync , autoremove , swap , upgrade-minimal .
Any other verb, and a run that changes nothing
.Pq Fl -simulate , Fl -dry-run , Fl -assumeno , Fl -downloadonly ,
takes no snapshot.
.Pp
A snapshot that fails is warned about and the command runs anyway: a busy
pool is no reason to refuse an install.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Under its own name, print the header comment and exit 0.
Under
.Nm apt
or
.Nm dnf ,
.Fl -help
goes to the real command.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/local/sbin/snapshot-create.sh
Takes the snapshot.
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/snapshots.log
Its log, named in the warning when it fails.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The real command's.
1 when the real command is missing, or
.Nm
is called by a name it does not know.
.Sh EXAMPLES
In a Fedora 44 container, with the snapshot script replaced by one that
records each call; the count is of snapshots taken:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dnf -y install no-such-package-xyz --assumeno      rc=1   snapshots=0
dnf install --downloadonly no-such-package-xyz -y  rc=1   snapshots=0
dnf -y --repo fedora install no-such-package-xyz   rc=1   snapshots=1 snapshot dnf-pre
dnf -q list installed bash                         rc=0   snapshots=0
.Ed
.Pp
In a Debian 13 container with
.Ql mokutil
held; apt's own CLI warning is left out:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# apt upgrade --simulate

kldload: 1 packages are HELD — the kernel, ZFS and NVIDIA are pinned
kldload: as one matched set, because the ZFS and NVIDIA modules are built
kldload: against this exact kernel. apt will list them as "kept back".
kldload: That is deliberate. It is not a fault, and unholding them is how
kldload: this machine stops booting.
kldload:
kldload:   see what is pinned   apt-mark showhold
kldload:   undo this upgrade    apt rollback
kldload:   move the kernel      documented in kldload-apply-platform-holds(8)

\&...
Reading package lists...
Building dependency tree...
Reading state information...
Calculating upgrade...
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
And what
.Cm rollback
hands on, with
.Xr kldload-rollback 8
replaced by a script that prints its arguments:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# dnf rollback
kldload-rollback last --no-reboot
# dnf rollback list
kldload-rollback list
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kldload-snapshots 7 ,
.Xr kldload-apply-platform-holds 8 ,
.Xr kldload-rollback 8 ,
.Xr kpkg 8
.Sh HISTORY
Until 2026-09-18 the verb was the first argument, so
.Ql dnf -y install
took no snapshot.
Until 2026-09-23 a bare
.Ql apt rollback
restarted the machine two seconds after staging.
On 2026-09-30, writing this page:
.Fl -assumeno
and
.Fl -downloadonly
runs took snapshots of nothing.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Only commands that go through the path are seen: anything that runs
.Pa /usr/bin/dnf
directly is not.
On Debian and Ubuntu apt's own hooks take the snapshot for those as well;
dnf has no such hook here.
.Pp
Snapshots are taken only when it runs as root.
